doc:    "Found by mutating cli/tests/conformance/lib/codegen/lib.buri at BURI_FUZZ_SEED=0x9e3779b9. `from\"\"export{///` / `d,d//` / `};` strands a doc line above one token of the export clause and an ordinary comment above another; both are moved under the declaration, and the first run printed them in the order they were written — `///` then `//` — while the second run swapped them, so `buri format` and `buri format --check` disagreed about the file forever. The same class as the wrapped match guard: a shape the printer emits that its own next read does not produce. The lexer keeps a token's `///` lines and its `//` comments in two vectors (lexer.rs's `Trivia`), so the order between them is already gone by the time the formatter sees them and *some* order has to be chosen; comments then docs is the one that reads right, because a section heading introduces the documentation under it. The bug is that the choice was applied per *emission* rather than per gap between two tokens, and comments concatenate while doc lines do not: a `///` printed by one emission and a `//` printed by the next come back swapped, because the next read of the file lexes the whole gap as one run. Three places printed a gap in more than one piece, and all three are fixed: `flush` now asks `trivia_doc` once about every run it drained rather than once per run; the module's per-declaration sweep, the one sweep in the file that is followed by more comments rather than by a token, leaves a run holding a `///` for the module's closing sweep, which is where the next read puts it anyway; and a run holding a `///` is no longer split off as a file header, because a header is printed at the top while the import it was written above sorts somewhere else. The printed shapes are pinned by cli/tests/formatting/comment_two_stranded_runs_come_back_as_one_run/ and cli/tests/formatting/comment_a_stranded_doc_comment_comes_back_last/. No existing formatting golden moved."
property: "roundtrip"
status: FIXED
